---
import "../styles/global.css";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="theme-color" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" content="#f3eedd" />
    <meta name="theme-color" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" content="#191d19" />
    <meta name="description" content="A beginner-friendly walkthrough of the Foxhole Forecast prompt and evidence flow." />
    <title>Prompt Flow · Foxhole Forecast</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="method-masthead">
      <div class="masthead__eyebrow">UNDER THE HOOD · SAME DATA, FROZEN CUTOFF, NO HINDSIGHT</div>
      <div class="method-hero page-width">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Inside one round</p>
          <h1>How a forecast<br /><span>gets made</span></h1>
          <p class="sublede">The whole pipeline in plain view: what the model saw, what it wrote, what it bet on, and how later game observations settle the call.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="method-back" href={basePath}>← Return to dashboard</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Prompt flow navigation">
      <div class="page-width">
        <a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a>
        <a href={`${basePath}predictions/`}>Round history</a>
        <a href={`${basePath}history/`}>Summary archive</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class="prompt-flow-page page-width">
      <section class="method-intro prompt-flow-intro">
        <p class="kicker">The basic idea</p>
        <p>No model sees a live feed. Each one gets a frozen snapshot, writes a short dispatch, picks the regions worth watching, then commits to eight timed predictions — plus four friendly recommendations that we deliberately don’t score.</p>
        <aside class="method-callout"><strong>Why unscored advice?</strong><span>We do not observe whether players followed the recommendations or what would have happened otherwise. Later activity therefore cannot establish that advice <em>caused</em> an outcome. We keep the recommendations for descriptive comparison; the valid predictions carry the scoring.</span></aside>
      </section>

      <section class="flow-section" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">01–07</p><h2 id="flow-title">One round, step by step</h2></div>
        <div class="flow-timeline">
          <article class="flow-step"><span>01</span><div><h3>The snapshot</h3><p>At a shared UTC cutoff, the model receives a compact whole-map overview of ownership and recent activity.</p><small>Models share the cutoff-time data; a model may also receive its own previous summary.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>02</span><div><h3>Dispatch and picks</h3><p>First it writes a short newspaper-style summary of the war, then names up to six regions whose detailed history it wants to inspect.</p><small>The summary is archived as the model’s briefing.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>03</span><div><h3>A closer look</h3><p>It receives deeper history for exactly those regions, drawn from the frozen source and excluding observations after the cutoff.</p><small>Which regions a model asks for is itself something we can inspect.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>04</span><div><h3>Eight timed calls</h3><p>It is asked for eight different bases, what will happen to each, an exact UTC time, a confidence, a timing spread, and evidence references. Valid bets are retained even if another bet fails validation.</p><small>Ranks 1–4 are asked to target the next six hours; ranks 5–8 the following day. Survivors keep their original ranks.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>05</span><div><h3>Four friendly tips</h3><p>It also answers four neutral prompts — where each faction should reinforce or attack. Advice only; these are never scored.</p><small>Each tip carries its own reasoning and evidence.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>06</span><div><h3>Recorded for audit</h3><p>Predictions, recommendations, cutoff, evidence, and received raw responses are stored. Exclusions and repairs are recorded explicitly. Delayed replays keep their frozen inputs and later generation times.</p><small>New explanations and derived analyses can reuse these records without another model call.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>07</span><div><h3>Reality votes</h3><p>We poll the official API on a nominal fifteen-minute cadence, subject to coverage gaps. Ownership changes are observed in intervals, and the eight calls are scored against what actually happened when a fair settlement is possible.</p><small>The unscored tips can be compared descriptively with later activity — but never treated as cause and effect.</small></div></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="prompt-example-section" aria-labelledby="examples-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">A real run, lightly edited</p><h2 id="examples-title">What the model actually returned</h2></div>
        <p class="method-caption">This is one continuous example from GPT-5.6 Luna in War 140, Round 13, using data frozen at 00:10 UTC on August 28. Quoted prose is the model’s own. An ellipsis (…) marks an omission; square brackets mark a small editorial clarification. Structured forecast fields are typeset as a sentence, but their values are unchanged.</p>
        <div class="prompt-example__source" aria-label="Example provenance">
          <strong>Example file</strong>
          <span>GPT-5.6 Luna · War 140 · Round 13</span>
          <span>Cutoff: 2026-08-28 00:10 UTC</span>
        </div>
        <div class="prompt-example-grid">
          <article class="prompt-example">
            <div class="prompt-example__top"><span>01 · Scout</span><strong>Make sense of the map</strong></div>
            <div class="prompt-example__body">
              <div class="prompt-example__explanation">
                <p>The first box is broad: a frozen summary of all 53 regions, 55 hours of available history, and Luna’s own previous dispatch. The model writes a fresh overview and chooses no more than six regions for the second pass.</p>
                <small>What goes forward</small>
                <p>Its six choices determine which regional histories appear in the detailed packet.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="prompt-example__output">
                <small>Headline</small>
                <h3>Lykos Line Shifts as Main Fronts Cool</h3>
                <small>Dispatch · excerpt</small>
                <blockquote>“Since the previous edition, Lykos Isle has moved to a four-base Warden, three-base Colonial balance, with two ownership events in the latest two hours and fighting still brisk on both casualty ledgers. … Deadlands continues as a divided and exceptionally costly sector—four Warden bases against seven Colonial—with seven ownership events and renewed enlistment activity.”</blockquote>
                <small>Regions it selected</small>
                <div class="prompt-chips"><span>Lykos Isle</span><span>Fisherman’s Row</span><span>Linn Mercy</span><span>Deadlands</span><span>Stonecradle</span><span>Stlican Shelf</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="prompt-example">
            <div class="prompt-example__top"><span>02 · Forecast</span><strong>Commit to eight calls</strong></div>
            <div class="prompt-example__body">
              <div class="prompt-example__explanation">
                <p>Luna then receives the detailed packet it requested: 56 strategic bases, 84 recent events, and 156 metric rows across those six regions. It returns eight structured calls. This is its first-ranked prediction.</p>
                <small>What the JSON means</small>
                <p>Confidence is the chance it assigns to the named event. The ±75-minute spread is its estimate of timing uncertainty—not an extra deadline.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="prompt-example__output">
                <small>Rank 1 of 8 · rendered from exact fields</small>
                <p class="prompt-call"><strong>42% confidence:</strong> <span class="base-identity base-identity--wardens">Callahan’s Gate · Town Base III</span> will be captured by the <span class="faction-name faction-name--colonials">Colonials</span> at <strong>02:25 UTC</strong>, with a <strong>±75 minute</strong> timing spread.</p>
                <small>Evidence it cited</small>
                <ul class="prompt-evidence-list">
                  <li><strong>8/10 relevance</strong><span>Warden casualties: +6,207 over six hours</span></li>
                  <li><strong>7/10 relevance</strong><span>Regional enlistments: +381 over six hours</span></li>
                  <li><strong>6/10 relevance</strong><span>Colonial-to-Warden casualty ratio: 0.8048</span></li>
                </ul>
                <p class="prompt-example__note">… [seven other timed predictions omitted here]</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="prompt-example">
            <div class="prompt-example__top"><span>03 · Advice</span><strong>Explain the strategy</strong></div>
            <div class="prompt-example__body">
              <div class="prompt-example__explanation">
                <p>The same response also contains one attack and one reinforce recommendation for each faction. They reuse the detailed packet and cite metrics, but do not enter the forecast score.</p>
                <small>Why keep it?</small>
                <p>It lets us inspect whether the model can turn the same evidence into recognizable strategic advice, even though later player behavior cannot validate the advice cleanly.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="prompt-example__output">
                <small>Warden reinforce recommendation · excerpt</small>
                <p class="prompt-call"><strong>Reinforce <span class="base-identity base-identity--wardens">Callahan’s Gate</span>.</strong></p>
                <blockquote>“[Callahan’s Gate] lies in the packet’s most intensely active region, with the largest recent enlistment and casualty volumes.”</blockquote>
                <small>Evidence it cited</small>
                <div class="prompt-evidence"><span>Enlistments +381 / 6h · 10/10</span><span>Colonial casualties +4,521 / 6h · 9/10</span><span>Warden casualties +6,207 / 6h · 9/10</span></div>
                <p class="prompt-example__note">… [three other faction recommendations omitted here]</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="prompt-access-section" aria-labelledby="prompt-access-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">Full source text</p><h2 id="prompt-access-title">Read the exact prompts</h2></div>
        <p>The page keeps things readable; the repository keeps the truth. The complete prompt text — including output rules and validation constraints — is public.</p>
        <div class="prompt-links"><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/blob/main/prompts/scout.md" rel="noreferrer">Open full scout prompt ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/blob/main/prompts/forecast.md" rel="noreferrer">Open full forecast prompt ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/tree/main/prompts" rel="noreferrer">Browse all prompt versions ↗</a></div>
        <p class="method-caption">The prompts ask only for conclusions supported by the snapshot. No model gets player communications, hidden inventories, or an in-game agent.</p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer><div class="page-width"><strong>FOXHOLE FORECAST</strong><a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a></div></footer>
  </body>
</html>
